CONTENT['SMTP Protocol']={
intro:'SMTP (Simple Mail Transfer Protocol) sends email from a mail client to a mail server and between mail servers across the Internet.',
obj:['Describe how an email travels from sender to recipient','Explain the roles of MUA, MTA and MDA','Trace an SMTP command dialogue','State the common SMTP ports'],
notes:`<h3>What is SMTP?</h3><p><b>SMTP</b> is a text-based application-layer <b>push</b> protocol that runs over TCP. It only <i>sends</i> mail; reading mail from a mailbox is done with POP3 or IMAP. SMTP was designed for 7-bit ASCII text, which is why MIME was added for attachments and other languages.</p>
<h3>Roles in email transmission</h3><ul><li><b>MUA (Mail User Agent):</b> the client the user types in, e.g. Outlook or a webmail page.</li><li><b>MTA (Mail Transfer Agent):</b> a mail server that accepts, queues and relays messages to other servers.</li><li><b>MDA (Mail Delivery Agent):</b> places the message into the recipient's mailbox.</li></ul>
<figure><svg viewBox="0 0 560 120" role="img" aria-label="Email flows from the sender client to the sending mail server by SMTP, to the receiving mail server by SMTP, and the recipient client retrieves it by POP3 or IMAP"><g fill="none" stroke="currentColor"><rect x="5" y="25" width="105" height="50" rx="8"/><rect x="150" y="25" width="105" height="50" rx="8"/><rect x="295" y="25" width="105" height="50" rx="8"/><rect x="440" y="25" width="105" height="50" rx="8"/><path d="M110 50H150M255 50H295M400 50H440"/></g><g fill="currentColor" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle"><text x="57" y="46">Sender</text><text x="57" y="62">client</text><text x="202" y="46">Sending</text><text x="202" y="62">mail server</text><text x="347" y="46">Receiving</text><text x="347" y="62">mail server</text><text x="492" y="46">Recipient</text><text x="492" y="62">client</text><text x="130" y="95">SMTP</text><text x="275" y="95">SMTP</text><text x="420" y="95">POP3/IMAP</text></g></svg><figcaption>Email transmission and retrieval: SMTP pushes the message server to server; the recipient pulls it with POP3 or IMAP.</figcaption></figure>
<h3>Working principle</h3><ol><li>The client submits the message to its outgoing mail server.</li><li>That server asks DNS for the <b>MX record</b> of the recipient's domain to find the receiving mail server.</li><li>It opens a TCP connection to that server and transfers the message with SMTP commands.</li><li>The receiving server stores the message in the recipient's mailbox.</li><li>The recipient later downloads it with POP3 or IMAP.</li></ol>
<h3>Common ports</h3><div class="tw"><table><tr><th>Port<th>Use</tr><tr><td>25<td>Server-to-server relay</tr><tr><td>587<td>Client submission with login (STARTTLS) – the standard choice</tr><tr><td>465<td>Submission over implicit TLS</tr></table></div>
<h3>Example dialogue</h3><pre>S: 220 mail.example.com ESMTP ready
C: EHLO client.example.org
S: 250 mail.example.com greets you
C: MAIL FROM:&lt;asha@example.org&gt;
S: 250 OK
C: RCPT TO:&lt;ravi@example.com&gt;
S: 250 OK
C: DATA
S: 354 End data with a line containing only a dot
C: Subject: Hello
C: See you in class.
C: .
S: 250 Message queued
C: QUIT
S: 221 Bye</pre><p><b>Explanation:</b> EHLO introduces the client, MAIL FROM and RCPT TO give the envelope sender and recipient, DATA sends the message, and the lone dot ends it. Reply codes starting with 2 mean success, 3 means "send more", 4 is a temporary failure and 5 is a permanent failure.</p>
<h3>Common mistakes</h3><ul><li>Thinking SMTP downloads mail to your phone; that is POP3/IMAP.</li><li>Confusing the envelope (MAIL FROM/RCPT TO) with the visible From/To headers.</li><li>Forgetting that DNS MX records decide where mail is delivered.</li></ul>`,
short:['SMTP = Simple Mail Transfer Protocol; sends mail (push), TCP','Ports: 25 relay, 587 submission, 465 implicit TLS','Commands: EHLO, MAIL FROM, RCPT TO, DATA, QUIT','Reply codes: 220 ready, 250 OK, 354 send data, 550 mailbox unavailable','MX record in DNS locates the receiving mail server','Retrieval is NOT SMTP: use POP3 or IMAP'],
cards:[['What does SMTP stand for?','Simple Mail Transfer Protocol.'],['Is SMTP a push or pull protocol?','Push: the sending side pushes the message to the next server.'],['Can SMTP read mail from a mailbox?','No. Mail is retrieved with POP3 or IMAP.'],['Which DNS record finds a domain\'s mail server?','The MX (Mail Exchanger) record.'],['Which port should email clients use to send mail?','Port 587 with STARTTLS and authentication (465 for implicit TLS; 25 is for server relay).'],['Name the main SMTP commands.','EHLO/HELO, MAIL FROM, RCPT TO, DATA, QUIT.'],['What does reply code 354 mean?','The server is ready for the message body, which ends with a line containing a single dot.'],['Why was MIME needed with SMTP?','SMTP was limited to 7-bit ASCII text; MIME encodes attachments and non-ASCII content.']],
quiz:[['Which protocol sends email between mail servers?',['POP3','IMAP','SMTP','FTP'],2,'SMTP transfers mail from client to server and between servers.'],['Which DNS record identifies the mail server of a domain?',['A','MX','CNAME','TXT'],1,'MX (Mail Exchanger) records list the servers that accept mail for a domain.'],['What does SMTP reply code 250 mean?',['Requested action completed','Start mail input','Service closing','Mailbox unavailable'],0,'2xx codes indicate success; 250 means the command was accepted.'],['Which command starts transfer of the message body?',['MAIL FROM','RCPT TO','DATA','QUIT'],2,'DATA tells the server the message content follows, ended by a single dot.'],['SMTP is best described as a…',['Pull protocol','Push protocol','Storage protocol','Naming protocol'],1,'SMTP pushes mail towards the destination server.']]};
CONTENT['POP3 Protocol']={
intro:'POP3 (Post Office Protocol version 3) lets a mail client download messages from a mail server to the local device.',
obj:['Explain how POP3 retrieves email','Describe the three POP3 states','Use basic POP3 commands','State advantages, limits and use cases'],
notes:`<h3>What is POP3?</h3><p><b>POP3</b> is a simple <b>pull</b> protocol used to retrieve email. The client logs in, downloads messages to the local computer and, by default, deletes them from the server. It uses TCP port <b>110</b>, or <b>995</b> when protected by TLS. Sending mail is still done by SMTP.</p>
<h3>Working principle (three states)</h3><div class="tw"><table><tr><th>State<th>What happens</tr><tr><td>Authorization<td>Client sends USER and PASS; server verifies the account.</tr><tr><td>Transaction<td>Client lists, reads (RETR) and marks (DELE) messages.</tr><tr><td>Update<td>After QUIT, the server permanently removes messages marked for deletion.</tr></table></div>
<h3>Main commands</h3><div class="tw"><table><tr><th>Command<th>Purpose</tr><tr><td>USER / PASS<td>Log in</tr><tr><td>STAT<td>Number of messages and total size</tr><tr><td>LIST<td>List messages with sizes</tr><tr><td>RETR n<td>Download message n</tr><tr><td>DELE n<td>Mark message n for deletion</tr><tr><td>QUIT<td>End session and apply deletions</tr></table></div>
<h3>Example session</h3><pre>C: USER asha
S: +OK
C: PASS ********
S: +OK Logged in
C: STAT
S: +OK 3 5120
C: RETR 1
S: +OK 1200 octets follow ...
C: DELE 1
S: +OK Message 1 marked
C: QUIT
S: +OK Bye</pre><p><b>Explanation:</b> servers answer +OK on success and -ERR on failure. STAT says there are 3 messages of 5120 bytes in total. The deletion becomes final only after QUIT.</p>
<h3>Advantages and limitations</h3><ul><li><b>Advantages:</b> simple, works offline after download, saves server storage, low bandwidth use.</li><li><b>Limitations:</b> mail lives on one device, no folder synchronisation, read/unread status is not shared, and losing the device can mean losing the mail.</li></ul>
<h3>Use cases</h3><p>Single-computer users, slow or unreliable connections, and mailboxes with small server quotas.</p>
<h3>Common mistakes</h3><ul><li>Assuming POP3 keeps mail synchronised across devices (that is IMAP).</li><li>Believing POP3 sends mail; it only retrieves.</li></ul>`,
short:['POP3 = retrieve email; pull; ports 110 / 995 (TLS)','States: Authorization → Transaction → Update','Commands: USER, PASS, STAT, LIST, RETR, DELE, QUIT','Responses: +OK or -ERR','Default: download then delete from server','Best for single-device, offline reading'],
cards:[['What does POP3 stand for?','Post Office Protocol version 3.'],['What is POP3 used for?','Downloading email from a mail server to a local client.'],['Default ports of POP3?','110 (plain) and 995 (POP3 over TLS).'],['Name the three POP3 states.','Authorization, Transaction and Update.'],['What does RETR do?','Downloads a specific message from the server.'],['When are DELE-marked messages removed?','In the Update state, after the client issues QUIT.'],['Main limitation of POP3?','No synchronisation across devices; mail is usually stored only on the downloading device.'],['What do POP3 servers reply on success and failure?','+OK for success and -ERR for failure.']],
quiz:[['POP3 is used to…',['Send email','Retrieve email','Resolve domain names','Encrypt web pages'],1,'POP3 downloads mail from the server to the client.'],['Which port does POP3 use by default?',['25','110','143','443'],1,'110 is plain POP3; 995 is POP3 over TLS.'],['Which command downloads a message?',['STAT','LIST','RETR','DELE'],2,'RETR n retrieves message number n.'],['When are deleted messages finally removed?',['Immediately on DELE','During Update state after QUIT','At login','Never'],1,'The Update state applies deletions when the session ends.'],['A key weakness of POP3 is…',['It cannot download mail','No multi-device synchronisation','It needs HTML','It uses UDP only'],1,'Mail is typically kept on one device, so other devices do not see the same state.']]};
CONTENT['IMAP Protocol']={
intro:'IMAP (Internet Message Access Protocol) lets users read and manage email that stays stored on the server, so every device sees the same mailbox.',
obj:['Explain server-side email storage','Describe folder and flag synchronisation','Compare IMAP, POP3 and SMTP','Choose the right protocol for a scenario'],
notes:`<h3>What is IMAP?</h3><p><b>IMAP</b> keeps messages on the mail server and lets clients view, search, organise and delete them remotely. Clients download headers first and fetch full messages or attachments only when needed. It uses TCP port <b>143</b>, or <b>993</b> with TLS.</p>
<h3>Key features</h3><ul><li><b>Server-side storage:</b> the server is the master copy.</li><li><b>Folder synchronisation:</b> Inbox, Sent, Drafts and custom folders look the same on every device.</li><li><b>Flags:</b> \\Seen, \\Answered, \\Deleted and \\Flagged are stored on the server.</li><li><b>Server-side search</b> and partial download of large messages.</li></ul>
<h3>SMTP vs POP3 vs IMAP</h3><div class="tw"><table><tr><th>Feature<th>SMTP<th>POP3<th>IMAP</tr><tr><td>Purpose<td>Send / relay mail<td>Download mail<td>Access and manage mail on server</tr><tr><td>Direction<td>Push<td>Pull<td>Pull</tr><tr><td>Ports<td>25, 587, 465<td>110, 995<td>143, 993</tr><tr><td>Mail stored<td>Temporarily in queues<td>Mostly on the client<td>On the server</tr><tr><td>Multi-device sync<td>Not applicable<td>Poor<td>Excellent</tr><tr><td>Offline use<td>No<td>Good<td>Needs cached copies</tr></table></div>
<h3>Example session</h3><pre>a1 LOGIN asha ********
a1 OK Logged in
a2 SELECT INBOX
* 12 EXISTS
a2 OK [READ-WRITE] Done
a3 FETCH 1 (FLAGS ENVELOPE)
a4 STORE 1 +FLAGS (\\Seen)
a5 LOGOUT</pre><p><b>Explanation:</b> every IMAP command starts with a tag (a1, a2…) so replies can be matched. SELECT opens the Inbox (12 messages), FETCH reads message data and STORE marks the message as read on the server.</p>
<h3>Advantages and limitations</h3><ul><li><b>Advantages:</b> consistent view on phone, laptop and webmail; less data lost if a device fails.</li><li><b>Limitations:</b> needs more server storage and a connection for most actions; more complex than POP3.</li></ul>
<h3>Common mistakes</h3><ul><li>Saying IMAP sends email; sending is always SMTP.</li><li>Confusing port 143 (IMAP) with 110 (POP3).</li></ul>`,
short:['IMAP = access mail stored on server; ports 143 / 993','Syncs folders and flags across devices','Fetches headers first; downloads bodies on demand','Commands are tagged: LOGIN, SELECT, FETCH, STORE, LOGOUT','POP3 downloads; IMAP synchronises; SMTP sends'],
cards:[['What does IMAP stand for?','Internet Message Access Protocol.'],['Where does IMAP keep email?','On the mail server; clients work with synchronised copies.'],['Default ports of IMAP?','143 (plain) and 993 (IMAP over TLS).'],['IMAP vs POP3 in one line?','IMAP syncs a server-side mailbox across devices; POP3 downloads mail, usually to one device.'],['What are IMAP flags?','Server-stored message states such as \\Seen, \\Answered, \\Deleted and \\Flagged.'],['Why do IMAP commands have tags?','The tag (like a1) lets the client match each server reply to its command.'],['Which protocol sends email when using IMAP?','SMTP. IMAP only accesses stored mail.'],['One drawback of IMAP?','It uses more server storage and usually needs a network connection.']],
quiz:[['Where is email stored in IMAP?',['Only on the client','On the server','On a DNS server','Nowhere'],1,'IMAP keeps the master copy on the server.'],['Which protocol best supports using one mailbox on phone and laptop?',['POP3','IMAP','SMTP','FTP'],1,'IMAP synchronises folders and flags across devices.'],['Port for IMAP over TLS?',['110','143','993','995'],2,'993 is IMAPS; 995 is POP3S.'],['What is the purpose of the tag in IMAP commands?',['Encryption','Match replies to commands','Select the port','Compress data'],1,'Tags such as a1 identify which command a reply belongs to.'],['Which task is NOT done by IMAP?',['Searching mail on server','Marking mail as read','Sending outgoing mail','Managing folders'],2,'Outgoing mail is sent by SMTP.']]};
CONTENT['MIME']={
intro:'MIME (Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions) lets email and web messages carry images, attachments, HTML and non-English text on top of protocols that were designed for plain ASCII.',
obj:['Explain why MIME is needed','Read MIME headers and content types','Describe multipart messages and encodings','Relate MIME to HTTP Content-Type'],
notes:`<h3>Why MIME?</h3><p>Original email (RFC 822 with SMTP) allowed only 7-bit ASCII text. <b>MIME</b> adds headers and encodings so a message can include accents, other scripts, images, audio, PDFs and several parts in a single message. SMTP itself is unchanged; MIME works inside the message.</p>
<h3>Important MIME headers</h3><div class="tw"><table><tr><th>Header<th>Meaning</tr><tr><td>MIME-Version<td>Declares the MIME version (1.0)</tr><tr><td>Content-Type<td>Type/subtype of the data, e.g. text/html</tr><tr><td>Content-Transfer-Encoding<td>How binary data is encoded as text (base64, quoted-printable, 7bit)</tr><tr><td>Content-Disposition<td>Show inline or treat as an attachment, with file name</tr></table></div>
<h3>Common content types</h3><div class="tw"><table><tr><th>Type<th>Examples</tr><tr><td>text<td>text/plain, text/html, text/css</tr><tr><td>image<td>image/png, image/jpeg</tr><tr><td>audio / video<td>audio/mpeg, video/mp4</tr><tr><td>application<td>application/pdf, application/json</tr><tr><td>multipart<td>multipart/mixed, multipart/alternative</tr></table></div>
<h3>Multipart messages</h3><p>A <b>multipart</b> message holds several body parts separated by a unique <b>boundary</b> string. <code>multipart/mixed</code> is used for text plus attachments; <code>multipart/alternative</code> carries the same content in several formats (plain text and HTML) so the client can pick the best one.</p>
<h3>Encodings</h3><ul><li><b>Base64:</b> converts binary into 64 printable characters; size grows by about 33%.</li><li><b>Quoted-printable:</b> keeps mostly-ASCII text readable and escapes special characters.</li><li><b>7bit / 8bit / binary:</b> data sent as is, if the servers allow it.</li></ul>
<h3>Example</h3><pre>MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: multipart/mixed; boundary="XYZ"

--XYZ
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="UTF-8"

Please find the notes attached.
--XYZ
Content-Type: application/pdf; name="notes.pdf"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: base64
Content-Disposition: attachment; filename="notes.pdf"

JVBERi0xLjQK...
--XYZ--</pre><p><b>Explanation:</b> the message has two parts. Part 1 is plain text; part 2 is a base64-encoded PDF shown as an attachment. The closing boundary ends with two extra hyphens.</p>
<h3>MIME on the web</h3><p>HTTP uses the same type names in its <code>Content-Type</code> header (for example text/html or application/json), and HTML file uploads use <code>multipart/form-data</code>.</p>
<h3>Common mistakes</h3><ul><li>Thinking MIME is a transport protocol; it is a message format standard.</li><li>Believing base64 is encryption; it is only encoding.</li></ul>`,
short:['MIME = extends email for non-ASCII text, attachments, multimedia','Headers: MIME-Version, Content-Type, Content-Transfer-Encoding, Content-Disposition','Type format: type/subtype (text/html, image/png)','multipart/mixed = attachments; multipart/alternative = same content, different formats','Base64 ≈ +33% size; it is encoding, not encryption','Same types used in HTTP Content-Type'],
cards:[['What does MIME stand for?','Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions.'],['Why was MIME introduced?','Email was limited to 7-bit ASCII text; MIME allows attachments, multimedia and non-ASCII text.'],['What does Content-Type specify?','The media type and subtype of the data, such as text/html or image/png.'],['What is a multipart message?','A message made of several parts separated by a boundary string.'],['multipart/mixed vs multipart/alternative?','Mixed combines different parts (text + attachments); alternative offers the same content in different formats.'],['What is base64 used for?','Encoding binary data as printable ASCII so it can pass through text-based mail systems.'],['Is base64 encryption?','No. It only changes representation and can be decoded by anyone.'],['Where else is MIME used besides email?','In HTTP Content-Type headers and multipart/form-data file uploads.']],
quiz:[['MIME was created mainly to…',['Encrypt email','Allow non-text content and non-ASCII text in email','Resolve domain names','Speed up TCP'],1,'MIME extends the original text-only message format.'],['Which header tells the type of data in a message part?',['MIME-Version','Content-Type','Subject','Received'],1,'Content-Type gives the type/subtype of the part.'],['Which type sends plain text and HTML versions of the same message?',['multipart/mixed','multipart/alternative','application/json','image/png'],1,'multipart/alternative lets the client choose the best version.'],['Base64 encoding makes data…',['Encrypted','About 33% larger','Smaller','Faster to load'],1,'Every 3 bytes become 4 characters.'],['Which HTTP feature uses MIME types?',['The Host header','The Content-Type header','The port number','The status line'],1,'HTTP reuses MIME media types in Content-Type.']]};
CONTENT['Domain Name System (DNS)']={
intro:'DNS is the Internet\'s phone book: it translates human-friendly domain names such as www.example.com into IP addresses and other records that computers need.',
obj:['Explain why DNS is needed','Describe the DNS hierarchy','Trace recursive resolution step by step','Identify A, AAAA, CNAME and MX records'],
notes:`<h3>What is DNS?</h3><p>Computers locate each other by IP address, but people remember names. The <b>Domain Name System</b> is a distributed, hierarchical database that maps <b>domain names</b> to <b>IP addresses</b> (and to mail servers, aliases and more). It mainly uses UDP (or TCP for large answers) on port <b>53</b>.</p>
<h3>DNS hierarchy</h3><ul><li><b>Root servers (.):</b> know which servers handle each top-level domain.</li><li><b>TLD servers:</b> handle .com, .org, .in and so on; they know the authoritative servers of each domain.</li><li><b>Authoritative name servers:</b> hold the real records for a domain such as example.com.</li><li><b>Recursive resolver:</b> run by an ISP or a public service; it does the searching on behalf of the client and caches answers.</li></ul>
<figure><svg viewBox="0 0 560 170" role="img" aria-label="A client asks a recursive resolver, which queries the root server, the TLD server and the authoritative server in turn, then returns the IP address"><g fill="none" stroke="currentColor"><rect x="5" y="55" width="110" height="60" rx="8"/><rect x="190" y="55" width="120" height="60" rx="8"/><rect x="395" y="6" width="160" height="40" rx="8"/><rect x="395" y="65" width="160" height="40" rx="8"/><rect x="395" y="124" width="160" height="40" rx="8"/><path d="M115 85H190M310 85L395 26M310 85H395M310 85L395 144"/></g><g fill="currentColor" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle"><text x="60" y="82">Client</text><text x="60" y="98">(stub resolver)</text><text x="250" y="82">Recursive</text><text x="250" y="98">resolver</text><text x="475" y="23">② Root server</text><text x="475" y="38" font-size="11">→ refers to TLD</text><text x="475" y="82">③ TLD server (.com)</text><text x="475" y="97" font-size="11">→ refers to authoritative</text><text x="475" y="141">④ Authoritative</text><text x="475" y="156" font-size="11">→ returns IP address</text><text x="152" y="76" font-size="11">① ↔ ⑤</text></g></svg><figcaption>Recursive resolution: ① the client asks the resolver; ② ③ ④ the resolver queries root, TLD and authoritative servers; ⑤ it returns (and caches) the answer.</figcaption></figure>
<h3>Step-by-step resolution of www.example.com</h3><ol><li>The browser and operating system check their caches and the hosts file.</li><li>If not found, the stub resolver sends a recursive query to the resolver.</li><li>The resolver asks a <b>root server</b>, which replies with the .com TLD servers.</li><li>It asks a <b>.com TLD server</b>, which replies with example.com's authoritative servers.</li><li>It asks the <b>authoritative server</b>, which returns the IP address.</li><li>The resolver caches the answer for its <b>TTL</b> (time to live) and replies to the client, which connects over HTTP.</li></ol>
<h3>Common DNS records</h3><div class="tw"><table><tr><th>Record<th>Purpose<th>Example</tr><tr><td>A<td>Name to IPv4 address<td>www → 203.0.113.10</tr><tr><td>AAAA<td>Name to IPv6 address<td>www → 2001:db8::10</tr><tr><td>CNAME<td>Alias to another name<td>blog → www.example.com</tr><tr><td>MX<td>Mail server for the domain (with priority)<td>10 mail.example.com</tr><tr><td>NS<td>Authoritative name servers<td>ns1.example.com</tr><tr><td>TXT<td>Free text, e.g. SPF/verification<td>"v=spf1 ..."</tr></table></div>
<h3>Example lookup</h3><pre>$ nslookup www.example.com
Name:    www.example.com
Address: 203.0.113.10</pre><p><b>Explanation:</b> the tool asked the resolver for the A record of the name and printed the IP address (a documentation example address). The browser then opens a TCP connection to that address.</p>
<h3>Caching and security</h3><p>Caching at the browser, OS and resolver reduces delay and load. Weaknesses include cache poisoning and spoofing; <b>DNSSEC</b> adds digital signatures to verify records, while DNS over HTTPS/TLS encrypts queries.</p>
<h3>Common mistakes</h3><ul><li>Confusing the recursive resolver with the authoritative server.</li><li>Pointing a CNAME at an IP address; it must point to a name.</li><li>Expecting a DNS change to be visible instantly; old TTLs keep cached copies.</li></ul>`,
short:['DNS = distributed hierarchical name → IP database; port 53','Hierarchy: Root → TLD → Authoritative','Recursive resolver does the work and caches (TTL)','A = IPv4, AAAA = IPv6, CNAME = alias, MX = mail, NS = name servers','Resolution order: cache → resolver → root → TLD → authoritative','DNSSEC signs records; DoH/DoT encrypt queries'],
cards:[['What is DNS?','The Domain Name System: a distributed hierarchical system that maps domain names to IP addresses and other records.'],['What does a recursive resolver do?','It performs the full lookup for the client by querying root, TLD and authoritative servers, and caches the result.'],['What do root servers return?','Referrals to the TLD servers (such as .com) for the requested name.'],['What is an authoritative name server?','The server that holds the official records for a domain and gives the final answer.'],['A vs AAAA record?','A maps a name to an IPv4 address; AAAA maps it to an IPv6 address.'],['What is a CNAME record?','An alias that points one name to another canonical name, never directly to an IP.'],['What does an MX record do?','Specifies the mail servers for a domain, with priority values.'],['What is TTL in DNS?','Time to live: how long a record may be cached before it must be looked up again.']],
quiz:[['Which server holds the actual records for example.com?',['Root server','TLD server','Authoritative name server','Proxy server'],2,'The authoritative server gives the final answer for the domain.'],['Which record maps a name to an IPv6 address?',['A','AAAA','MX','CNAME'],1,'AAAA is the IPv6 address record.'],['In recursive resolution, who queries root, TLD and authoritative servers?',['The browser','The recursive resolver','The web server','The router'],1,'The resolver performs the lookup on the client\'s behalf.'],['What does a CNAME record define?',['Mail server','Alias for another name','IPv4 address','Name server'],1,'CNAME points a name to a canonical name.'],['What does TTL control?',['Packet size','How long a record can be cached','Port number','Encryption strength'],1,'After TTL expires the record must be fetched again.']]};
CONTENT['World Wide Web (WWW)']={
intro:'The World Wide Web is a system of interlinked pages and resources that you access with a browser. It is one service that runs on top of the Internet.',
obj:['Differentiate the Internet from the WWW','Define web pages, websites and hyperlinks','Compare static and dynamic websites','Explain the client-server model'],
notes:`<h3>Definition and purpose</h3><p>The <b>World Wide Web (WWW)</b> is an information system in which documents and other resources are identified by <b>URLs</b>, linked by <b>hyperlinks</b>, and transferred by <b>HTTP</b>. It was proposed by Tim Berners-Lee at CERN around 1989–1991 so that researchers could share linked documents.</p>
<h3>Internet vs WWW</h3><div class="tw"><table><tr><th>Aspect<th>Internet<th>World Wide Web</tr><tr><td>Nature<td>Global network of networks (infrastructure)<td>Collection of linked resources (a service)</tr><tr><td>Main protocols<td>TCP/IP, DNS, SMTP, FTP<td>HTTP/HTTPS, HTML, URL</tr><tr><td>Needs<td>Cables, routers, ISPs<td>Internet, browsers, web servers</tr><tr><td>Other services<td>Email, file transfer, VoIP<td>Web pages and web apps</tr></table></div>
<h3>Key terms</h3><ul><li><b>Web page:</b> one HTML document.</li><li><b>Website:</b> a collection of related pages under one domain.</li><li><b>Hyperlink:</b> a clickable reference that moves the user to another resource; following links is called <b>navigation</b>.</li><li><b>URL:</b> the address of a resource.</li></ul>
<h3>Anatomy of a URL</h3><div class="tw"><table><tr><th>Part<th>Example</tr><tr><td>Scheme<td>https</tr><tr><td>Host<td>www.example.com</tr><tr><td>Port (optional)<td>:443</tr><tr><td>Path<td>/courses/web.html</tr><tr><td>Query<td>?unit=3</tr><tr><td>Fragment<td>#ajax</tr></table></div>
<h3>Static vs dynamic websites</h3><div class="tw"><table><tr><th>Feature<th>Static<th>Dynamic</tr><tr><td>Content<td>Same for every visitor<td>Generated per request (database, user)</tr><tr><td>Technology<td>HTML, CSS, JS files<td>Server-side code + database</tr><tr><td>Speed and cost<td>Fast, cheap to host<td>More processing and cost</tr><tr><td>Examples<td>Portfolio, documentation<td>Online shop, social network</tr></table></div>
<h3>Client-server model</h3><p>The <b>client</b> (browser) requests a resource; the <b>server</b> stores and sends it. Workflow: the user types a URL → DNS finds the server → the browser sends an HTTP request → the server returns HTML → the browser renders it and fetches linked CSS, JS and images.</p>
<h3>Real-world examples</h3><p>Reading news, online banking, e-learning portals and video streaming sites are all web applications reachable through the WWW.</p>
<h3>Common mistakes</h3><ul><li>Using "Internet" and "Web" as synonyms.</li><li>Thinking a dynamic website needs no server; its pages are produced by server-side code.</li></ul>`,
short:['WWW = linked resources (URL + HTTP + HTML) on top of the Internet','Inventor: Tim Berners-Lee, CERN','Internet = infrastructure; Web = one service','URL = scheme + host + port + path + query + fragment','Static = fixed files; dynamic = generated per request','Client-server: browser requests, server responds'],
cards:[['What is the World Wide Web?','An information system of interlinked resources identified by URLs and transferred using HTTP.'],['Who proposed the WWW?','Tim Berners-Lee at CERN (proposal 1989, first web server 1990–91).'],['Internet vs Web in one line?','The Internet is the global network; the Web is a service that runs on it.'],['What is a hyperlink?','A reference in a document that takes the user to another resource when activated.'],['What is a URL?','Uniform Resource Locator: the address of a resource, e.g. https://www.example.com/index.html.'],['Static vs dynamic website?','Static sites serve fixed files; dynamic sites generate content for each request using server-side code and data.'],['Website vs web page?','A web page is one document; a website is a collection of related pages.'],['What are the client and server roles?','The client requests resources; the server stores them and sends responses.']],
quiz:[['Which statement is correct?',['The Web and the Internet are identical','The Web is a service that runs on the Internet','The Internet runs on the Web','The Web is hardware'],1,'The Internet is the infrastructure; the Web is one service using it.'],['Who proposed the World Wide Web?',['Vint Cerf','Tim Berners-Lee','Bill Gates','Linus Torvalds'],1,'Tim Berners-Lee developed the Web at CERN.'],['Which part of a URL tells the protocol?',['Path','Scheme','Fragment','Query'],1,'The scheme (https, http, ftp) names the protocol.'],['A dynamic website…',['Never changes','Generates content per request','Has no server','Uses only HTML files'],1,'Dynamic sites build pages using server-side logic and data.'],['In the client-server model, the browser is the…',['Server','Client','Router','Resolver'],1,'The browser requests resources from servers.']]};
CONTENT['Web Browsers']={
intro:'A web browser is the client application that requests web resources, interprets HTML, CSS and JavaScript, and displays pages to the user.',
obj:['Describe browser architecture','Explain the rendering pipeline (DOM, CSSOM)','Distinguish rendering and JavaScript engines','Outline caching and security features'],
notes:`<h3>What does a browser do?</h3><p>A browser sends HTTP requests, receives responses, turns HTML/CSS/JS into a visual page, runs scripts and stores data such as cookies and cache. Popular browsers are Chrome, Edge, Firefox, Safari and Opera.</p>
<h3>Browser architecture</h3><div class="tw"><table><tr><th>Component<th>Role</tr><tr><td>User interface<td>Address bar, tabs, buttons, bookmarks</tr><tr><td>Browser engine<td>Coordinates the UI and the rendering engine</tr><tr><td>Rendering engine<td>Parses HTML/CSS and paints the page (Blink, WebKit, Gecko)</tr><tr><td>JavaScript engine<td>Compiles and runs JS (V8, SpiderMonkey, JavaScriptCore)</tr><tr><td>Networking<td>HTTP/HTTPS requests, DNS, caching</tr><tr><td>Data storage<td>Cookies, localStorage, IndexedDB, cache</tr></table></div>
<h3>Rendering pipeline</h3><figure><svg viewBox="0 0 560 70" role="img" aria-label="Rendering pipeline: DOM and CSSOM, render tree, layout, paint, composite"><g fill="none" stroke="currentColor"><rect x="3" y="15" width="74" height="40" rx="6"/><rect x="99" y="15" width="74" height="40" rx="6"/><rect x="195" y="15" width="74" height="40" rx="6"/><rect x="291" y="15" width="74" height="40" rx="6"/><rect x="387" y="15" width="74" height="40" rx="6"/><rect x="483" y="15" width="74" height="40" rx="6"/><path d="M77 35H99M173 35H195M269 35H291M365 35H387M461 35H483"/></g><g fill="currentColor" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle"><text x="40" y="39">HTML→DOM</text><text x="136" y="39">CSS→CSSOM</text><text x="232" y="39">Render tree</text><text x="328" y="39">Layout</text><text x="424" y="39">Paint</text><text x="520" y="39">Composite</text></g></svg><figcaption>The browser builds the DOM from HTML and the CSSOM from CSS, merges them into a render tree, calculates positions (layout), draws pixels (paint) and combines layers (composite).</figcaption></figure>
<ul><li><b>DOM:</b> tree of the page\'s elements, which JavaScript can change.</li><li><b>CSSOM:</b> tree of CSS rules and computed styles.</li><li>JavaScript can modify both, which triggers layout and paint again.</li></ul>
<h3>Caching</h3><p>Browsers store copies of files so repeat visits are faster. Headers such as <code>Cache-Control</code>, <code>ETag</code> and <code>Last-Modified</code> tell the browser how long a copy is valid and whether it must be re-validated (a 304 Not Modified response avoids re-downloading).</p>
<h3>Security features</h3><ul><li><b>Same-origin policy:</b> scripts from one origin cannot freely read data from another.</li><li><b>Sandboxing:</b> each tab/process runs with limited privileges.</li><li><b>HTTPS checks:</b> certificate validation and warnings.</li><li><b>Safe-browsing lists</b> and permission prompts (camera, location).</li></ul>
<h3>Common mistakes</h3><ul><li>Confusing the browser with the search engine.</li><li>Mixing the rendering engine (layout) with the JavaScript engine (scripts).</li><li>Assuming CSS or JS files do not affect first paint; blocking scripts delay rendering.</li></ul>`,
short:['Browser = HTTP client + renderer + script runner','Engines: Blink (Chrome, Edge), Gecko (Firefox), WebKit (Safari)','JS engines: V8, SpiderMonkey, JavaScriptCore','Pipeline: DOM + CSSOM → render tree → layout → paint → composite','Cache validated by Cache-Control, ETag, Last-Modified','Security: same-origin policy, sandbox, HTTPS checks'],
cards:[['What is a web browser?','Client software that requests, retrieves and renders web resources and runs web scripts.'],['What does the rendering engine do?','Parses HTML and CSS and lays out and paints the page (e.g. Blink, Gecko, WebKit).'],['Name three JavaScript engines.','V8 (Chrome/Edge), SpiderMonkey (Firefox), JavaScriptCore (Safari).'],['What is the DOM?','Document Object Model: a tree representation of the page that scripts can read and modify.'],['What is the CSSOM?','The tree of CSS rules and computed styles used with the DOM to build the render tree.'],['Order the rendering pipeline.','DOM + CSSOM → render tree → layout → paint → composite.'],['What is the same-origin policy?','A rule that restricts how documents or scripts from one origin interact with resources from another.'],['What does HTTP 304 mean for caching?','Not Modified: the cached copy is still valid, so no new body is sent.']],
quiz:[['Which component runs JavaScript code?',['Rendering engine','JavaScript engine','Network layer','Cookie jar'],1,'Engines like V8 compile and execute JavaScript.'],['Which is a rendering engine?',['V8','Gecko','SQLite','Node.js'],1,'Gecko (Firefox) is a rendering engine; V8 is a JS engine.'],['The render tree is built from…',['HTML only','DOM and CSSOM','JavaScript only','HTTP headers'],1,'Visible DOM nodes are combined with computed styles.'],['What does a 304 response save?',['Re-downloading unchanged content','DNS lookup','TLS handshake','Cookie storage'],0,'The browser reuses its cached copy.'],['The same-origin policy primarily protects…',['Image quality','Data from other sites','CSS rules','Server hardware'],1,'It stops scripts from reading data across origins without permission.']]};
CONTENT['Web Servers']={
intro:'A web server is software (running on a computer) that accepts HTTP requests from clients and returns web pages, files or generated content.',
obj:['Explain how a web server handles requests','Differentiate static and dynamic content delivery','Compare a web server with an application server','Name popular servers such as Apache and Nginx'],
notes:`<h3>Definition and functionality</h3><p>A <b>web server</b> listens on port 80 (HTTP) or 443 (HTTPS), accepts connections, maps each URL to a resource, and sends an HTTP response. It also logs requests, enforces access rules, compresses responses, and can terminate TLS.</p>
<h3>Static and dynamic delivery</h3><ul><li><b>Static:</b> the server reads a file (HTML, CSS, image) from disk and sends it unchanged.</li><li><b>Dynamic:</b> the server passes the request to code (PHP, Node.js, Python, Java) or an application server, which may query a database and builds the page.</li></ul>
<h3>Request lifecycle</h3><ol><li>The browser resolves the domain with DNS and opens a TCP (and TLS) connection.</li><li>The server parses the request line, headers and body.</li><li>It maps the URL to a file or handler and checks permissions.</li><li>Static content is read; dynamic content is generated by the application.</li><li>The server builds the response (status line, headers, body) and sends it.</li><li>It writes a log entry and keeps the connection open or closes it.</li></ol>
<h3>Web server vs application server</h3><div class="tw"><table><tr><th>Aspect<th>Web server<th>Application server</tr><tr><td>Main job<td>Serve HTTP content, static files, routing<td>Run business logic and components</tr><tr><td>Content<td>Mostly static<td>Dynamic</tr><tr><td>Examples<td>Apache HTTP Server, Nginx<td>Tomcat, WildFly, Node/Express app</tr><tr><td>Typical setup<td>Front end / reverse proxy<td>Behind the web server, talks to the database</tr></table></div>
<h3>Browser vs web server</h3><div class="tw"><table><tr><th>Browser<th>Web server</tr><tr><td>Client; sends requests<td>Serves responses</tr><tr><td>Renders HTML, runs JS<td>Delivers files or generated data</tr><tr><td>Runs on the user\'s device<td>Runs on a host reachable on the network</tr></table></div>
<h3>Popular servers</h3><ul><li><b>Apache HTTP Server:</b> modular, supports per-directory settings (.htaccess).</li><li><b>Nginx:</b> event-driven, excellent for many concurrent connections, reverse proxy and load balancing.</li><li><b>Microsoft IIS:</b> web server for Windows.</li></ul>
<h3>Example Nginx configuration</h3><pre>server {
  listen 80;
  server_name example.com;
  root /var/www/html;
  location / {
    try_files $uri $uri/ =404;
  }
}</pre><p><b>Explanation:</b> the server listens on port 80 for example.com, serves files from /var/www/html, and returns 404 if no matching file exists. <b>Result:</b> a request for /about.html sends /var/www/html/about.html.</p>
<h3>Common mistakes</h3><ul><li>Treating "server" as only hardware; here it is software too.</li><li>Thinking a web server itself runs your PHP/Python code; it hands it to an interpreter or app server.</li><li>Leaving directory listing or default pages exposed.</li></ul>`,
short:['Web server = software that answers HTTP/HTTPS requests (ports 80/443)','Static = send file as is; dynamic = generated by app code','Apache: modular; Nginx: event-driven, reverse proxy','App server runs business logic; web server routes and serves','Lifecycle: connect → parse → map → generate → respond → log'],
cards:[['What is a web server?','Software that listens for HTTP requests and returns web content such as files or generated pages.'],['Default ports of a web server?','80 for HTTP and 443 for HTTPS.'],['Static vs dynamic content delivery?','Static files are sent unchanged; dynamic content is generated by server-side code per request.'],['Name two popular web servers.','Apache HTTP Server and Nginx (also Microsoft IIS).'],['What is Nginx known for?','An event-driven design handling many connections, plus reverse proxying and load balancing.'],['Web server vs application server?','The web server handles HTTP and static content; the application server runs business logic and dynamic components.'],['What is a reverse proxy?','A server that receives client requests and forwards them to backend servers, returning their responses.'],['What does a web server log?','Each request: client IP, time, URL, status code and bytes sent.']],
quiz:[['Which port does HTTPS use by default?',['80','443','25','21'],1,'HTTPS uses port 443.'],['Which is a web server?',['Nginx','MySQL','Chrome','Git'],0,'Nginx is a web server and reverse proxy.'],['A dynamic response is typically produced by…',['A file copied unchanged','Server-side code, often with a database','The browser cache','DNS'],1,'Dynamic pages are generated per request.'],['What does a 404 from the server mean?',['Server crashed','Resource not found','Access forbidden','Redirect'],1,'404 means the requested resource does not exist.'],['Which component usually sits behind the web server to run business logic?',['Application server','Router','Resolver','Mail agent'],0,'Application servers such as Tomcat run application code.']]};
CONTENT['History of the Internet']={
intro:'The Internet grew from a small research network in 1969 into the global network of networks we use today. Knowing the milestones explains why TCP/IP, DNS and the Web exist.',
obj:['Explain the origin of computer networking and packet switching','Describe ARPANET and its role','Explain how TCP/IP became the standard','List key milestones up to the World Wide Web'],
notes:`<h3>Origins of computer networking</h3><p>In the 1960s computers were large, expensive and isolated. Researchers developed <b>packet switching</b>: data is split into small packets that travel independently and are reassembled at the destination. It is more efficient and more robust than a dedicated circuit, because packets can take another route if a link fails.</p>
<h3>ARPANET</h3><p><b>ARPANET</b> was funded by the US Department of Defense's research agency (ARPA). Its first message, between UCLA and the Stanford Research Institute, was sent in October 1969, and by the end of that year four universities were connected. It showed that different computers could exchange data over a packet-switched network. Email appeared in 1971 and soon became its most popular use.</p>
<h3>Evolution of TCP/IP</h3><p>Early networks used incompatible protocols. Vint Cerf and Bob Kahn designed <b>TCP/IP</b> (published 1974) so that many networks could be joined: <b>IP</b> addresses and routes packets, <b>TCP</b> provides reliable, ordered delivery. On 1 January 1983 ARPANET switched to TCP/IP, which is often treated as the birth of the modern Internet. DNS followed in the mid-1980s to replace manual host tables.</p>
<h3>Development of the World Wide Web</h3><p>In 1989 Tim Berners-Lee at CERN proposed a system of linked documents. By 1990–91 he had created HTML, HTTP, URLs, the first browser and the first web server. The <b>Mosaic</b> browser (1993) made the Web popular with its images and easy interface, and commercial use of the Internet grew rapidly after the mid-1990s.</p>
<figure><svg viewBox="0 0 560 120" role="img" aria-label="Timeline: 1969 ARPANET, 1971 email, 1983 TCP/IP adopted, 1991 World Wide Web, 1993 Mosaic browser"><line x1="20" y1="45" x2="540" y2="45" stroke="currentColor"/><g fill="currentColor"><circle cx="50" cy="45" r="5"/><circle cx="165" cy="45" r="5"/><circle cx="280" cy="45" r="5"/><circle cx="395" cy="45" r="5"/><circle cx="505" cy="45" r="5"/></g><g fill="currentColor" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle"><text x="50" y="72" font-weight="bold">1969</text><text x="50" y="88">ARPANET</text><text x="165" y="72" font-weight="bold">1971</text><text x="165" y="88">Email</text><text x="280" y="72" font-weight="bold">1983</text><text x="280" y="88">TCP/IP adopted</text><text x="395" y="72" font-weight="bold">1991</text><text x="395" y="88">World Wide Web</text><text x="505" y="72" font-weight="bold">1993</text><text x="505" y="88">Mosaic</text></g></svg><figcaption>Key milestones in the history of the Internet and the Web.</figcaption></figure>
<h3>Timeline</h3><div class="tw"><table><tr><th>Period<th>Milestone</tr><tr><td>1960s<td>Packet switching developed</tr><tr><td>1969<td>ARPANET's first links</tr><tr><td>1971<td>Network email</tr><tr><td>1974–1983<td>TCP/IP designed, then adopted by ARPANET</tr><tr><td>1980s<td>DNS; NSFNET connects universities</tr><tr><td>1989–1991<td>World Wide Web created at CERN</tr><tr><td>1993–1995<td>Mosaic, Netscape; commercial Internet grows</tr><tr><td>2000s onward<td>Broadband, social web, mobile, cloud, IoT</tr></table></div>
<h3>Common mistakes</h3><ul><li>Saying the Web and the Internet began together; the Internet is older.</li><li>Believing ARPANET was the whole Internet; it was one of the networks that merged into it.</li></ul>`,
short:['Packet switching: data split into independently routed packets','ARPANET (1969) = first major packet-switched network','TCP/IP adopted by ARPANET on 1 Jan 1983','Cerf and Kahn designed TCP/IP; Berners-Lee created the Web (CERN, 1989–91)','Mosaic (1993) popularised the Web','IP = addressing/routing; TCP = reliable delivery'],
cards:[['What is packet switching?','Splitting data into packets that are routed independently and reassembled at the destination.'],['What was ARPANET?','An early packet-switched research network funded by ARPA, first linked in 1969.'],['Who designed TCP/IP?','Vint Cerf and Bob Kahn.'],['When did ARPANET adopt TCP/IP?','On 1 January 1983.'],['Who created the World Wide Web?','Tim Berners-Lee at CERN (proposal 1989, first server 1990–91).'],['Why did Mosaic matter?','Its graphical, easy interface with inline images made the Web popular.'],['TCP vs IP?','IP addresses and routes packets; TCP provides reliable, ordered delivery.'],['Which service became ARPANET\'s most popular early use?','Electronic mail (introduced in 1971).']],
quiz:[['Packet switching means…',['One dedicated line per call','Splitting data into independently routed packets','Sending only text','Using satellites only'],1,'Packets are routed independently and reassembled.'],['Which network is regarded as the ancestor of the Internet?',['ARPANET','Ethernet','Bluetooth','Fibre Channel'],0,'ARPANET was the first major packet-switched network.'],['Who designed TCP/IP?',['Berners-Lee and Jobs','Cerf and Kahn','Gates and Allen','Torvalds and Stallman'],1,'Vint Cerf and Bob Kahn.'],['The World Wide Web was created at…',['MIT','CERN','Bell Labs','Google'],1,'Tim Berners-Lee worked at CERN.'],['Which protocol provides reliable ordered delivery?',['IP','UDP','TCP','ARP'],2,'TCP retransmits lost data and orders segments.']]};
CONTENT['HTML Fundamentals']={
intro:'HTML (HyperText Markup Language) is the standard language that describes the structure and meaning of web page content.',
obj:['Write a valid HTML5 document skeleton','Use elements, tags and attributes correctly','Use headings, paragraphs and links','Explain why semantic HTML matters'],
notes:`<h3>Introduction</h3><p><b>HTML</b> is a <i>markup</i> language, not a programming language: it labels content (heading, paragraph, link, image) with <b>tags</b>, and the browser decides how to display it. The current standard is HTML5, maintained as a living standard. CSS controls appearance and JavaScript adds behaviour.</p>
<h3>Basic document structure</h3><pre>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html lang="en"&gt;
&lt;head&gt;
  &lt;meta charset="UTF-8"&gt;
  &lt;meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"&gt;
  &lt;title&gt;My First Page&lt;/title&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
  &lt;h1&gt;Welcome&lt;/h1&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;Read the &lt;a href="notes.html"&gt;notes&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;</pre><p><b>Explanation:</b> <code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;</code> selects standards mode; <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> holds metadata (character set, title) that is not displayed on the page; <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> holds visible content. <b>Output:</b> a large heading "Welcome" and a paragraph containing a clickable link.</p>
<h3>Elements, tags and attributes</h3><div class="tw"><table><tr><th>Term<th>Meaning<th>Example</tr><tr><td>Tag<td>Markup in angle brackets<td>&lt;p&gt; and &lt;/p&gt;</tr><tr><td>Element<td>Start tag + content + end tag<td>&lt;p&gt;Hello&lt;/p&gt;</tr><tr><td>Attribute<td>Extra information in the start tag<td>href="notes.html"</tr><tr><td>Void element<td>No content or end tag<td>&lt;br&gt;, &lt;img&gt;, &lt;hr&gt;, &lt;meta&gt;</tr></table></div>
<h3>Headings, paragraphs and links</h3><ul><li><b>Headings</b> <code>h1</code>–<code>h6</code> show the outline; use one main <code>h1</code> and do not skip levels just for size.</li><li><b>Paragraph</b> <code>p</code> groups text; browsers collapse extra spaces.</li><li><b>Link</b> <code>a href="..."</code> navigates to another resource; <code>target="_blank"</code> with <code>rel="noopener"</code> opens a new tab safely.</li></ul>
<h3>Semantic HTML</h3><p>Semantic elements describe meaning, helping accessibility tools, search engines and developers.</p><div class="tw"><table><tr><th>Non-semantic<th>Semantic<th>Purpose</tr><tr><td>&lt;div&gt;<td>&lt;header&gt;, &lt;nav&gt;<td>Page or section header; navigation links</tr><tr><td>&lt;div&gt;<td>&lt;main&gt;, &lt;section&gt;, &lt;article&gt;<td>Main content, themed group, self-contained item</tr><tr><td>&lt;div&gt;<td>&lt;aside&gt;, &lt;footer&gt;<td>Side content; footer information</tr></table></div>
<h3>Common mistakes</h3><ul><li>Forgetting the DOCTYPE or <code>lang</code> attribute.</li><li>Using headings only to make text big (use CSS).</li><li>Overlapping tags such as <code>&lt;b&gt;&lt;i&gt;text&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/i&gt;</code>.</li><li>Using <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> everywhere instead of semantic elements.</li></ul>`,
short:['HTML = markup language for structure; CSS = style; JS = behaviour','Skeleton: DOCTYPE, html, head, body','Element = start tag + content + end tag; attributes live in the start tag','Void elements: br, img, hr, meta, input','Headings h1–h6; p; a href','Semantic tags: header, nav, main, section, article, aside, footer'],
cards:[['What does HTML stand for?','HyperText Markup Language.'],['Is HTML a programming language?','No. It is a markup language that structures content.'],['What is the purpose of the DOCTYPE?','It tells the browser to use standards mode for HTML5.'],['What goes inside the head element?','Metadata such as title, character set, viewport and links to CSS; it is not displayed on the page.'],['What is an HTML attribute?','A name–value pair in the start tag that gives extra information, e.g. href or alt.'],['Give four void elements.','br, hr, img and meta (also input and link).'],['What is semantic HTML?','Using elements whose names describe their meaning, such as nav, main and article.'],['Which tag creates a hyperlink?','The anchor element a with an href attribute.']],
quiz:[['Which element contains the visible page content?',['head','body','title','meta'],1,'body holds everything displayed in the viewport.'],['Which is a void element?',['p','div','br','h1'],2,'br has no content or end tag.'],['What is the correct way to start an HTML5 document?',['<html5>','<!DOCTYPE html>','<xml>','<doctype>'],1,'The short DOCTYPE triggers standards mode.'],['Which element is semantic?',['div','span','nav','b'],2,'nav represents navigation links.'],['The href attribute belongs to…',['img','a','p','table'],1,'href gives the link target of an anchor.']]};
